\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {F.36}{\ignorespaces Navier--Stokes 2D, phase-2 gates in four settings: the $K=16$ head on the full-rank $R=256$ bank, the $K=32$ head on the full-rank $R=512$ bank, the same $K=16$ recipe on a family of intrinsic dimension 8{} instead of 14{} (3{} modes; job 3808498, A100 (pax105)), and the $K=16$ recipe at $4\times $ the training data (2048{} trajectories: 512{} gated plus 1536{} extra, same family; last row, job 3808495, A100 (pax106)) (jobs 3787319 ($K{=}16$, $R{=}256$), 3787320 ($K{=}32$, $R{=}512$), 3808498 ($K{=}16$, $R{=}256$, family dimension 8), 3808495 ($K{=}16$, $R{=}256$, 2048 trajectories); full-order solver and dataset certified in job 3780151). Every gate passes except the held-out oracle, whose pre-registered bar was a $2.0\times $ margin over POD at matched dimension (ns2d/DESIGN.md H-ORACLE gate: oracle median <= 1/2 x POD-K held-out, i.e. ratio >= 2.0; pre-registered at commit 1281ff73 (2026-09-17 00:55), before jobs 3783796/3787319/3787320); the ratio is 1.15--1.46{} across the 4{} settings. No confirmatory ladder on a passing head was run; the exploratory phase-3 ladder after this failed gate is in Table~\ref {tab:ns-ladder}. With $4\times $ the data the training reconstruction rises from 5.0345{} to 12.3875\,\% and the held-out/training ratio falls from 4.0{} to 1.3. These are mixed-normalisation ratios: training and held-out errors use different denominators, so the change does not establish a shift from generalisation to capacity limits. The held-out POD/oracle ratios remain comparable (0.52{} at $t=0$, 1.39{} on evolved times). The $4\times $-data arm's B-DATA gate passed by the pre-registered value path (a hash mismatch on its node, at $10^{-16}$ on the archived trajectories). On the lower-dimensional family every error falls 3.3--4.4$\times $ (POD-16 3.3, oracle 3.8, bank floor 4.4) while the ratio stays at 1.39{} (1.83{} at $t=0$, 1.44{} on evolved times) and the held-out/training ratio at 3.8{} against 4.0. Oracle values are upper bounds: 12{} ($K{=}16$), 18{} ($K{=}32$), 4{} (family dimension 8) and 6{} (2048{} trajectories) held-out fits at $256^2$ hit the LM budget, which biases the ratio downward, so the result establishes failure to clear the gate with the available fits, not a proof that the globally best manifold fit cannot clear it.}}{37}{table.caption.71}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ns}{{F.36}{37}{Navier--Stokes 2D, phase-2 gates in four settings: the $K=16$ head on the full-rank $R=256$ bank, the $K=32$ head on the full-rank $R=512$ bank, the same $K=16$ recipe on a family of intrinsic dimension \nNsFamDimLow {} instead of \nNsFamDimBase {} (\nNsFamLowModes {} modes; job \provNsFamJob , \provNsFamGpu ), and the $K=16$ recipe at $\nNsDataFactor \times $ the training data (\nNsDataTrainN {} trajectories: \nNsDataTrainBase {} gated plus \nNsDataTrainExtra {} extra, same family; last row, job \provNsDataJob , \provNsDataGpu ) (jobs \provNsJobs ; full-order solver and dataset certified in job \provNsFomJob ). Every gate passes except the held-out oracle, whose pre-registered bar was a $\nNsOracleBar \times $ margin over POD at matched dimension (\provNsBarSource ); the ratio is \nNsCellRatioMin --\nNsCellRatioMax {} across the \nNsCellArms {} settings. No confirmatory ladder on a passing head was run; the exploratory phase-3 ladder after this failed gate is in Table~\ref {tab:ns-ladder}. With $\nNsDataFactor \times $ the data the training reconstruction rises from \nNsDataBaseTrainRecon {} to \nNsDataTrainRecon \,\% and the held-out/training ratio falls from \nNsDataBaseGap {} to \nNsDataHeldoutOverTrain . These are mixed-normalisation ratios: training and held-out errors use different denominators, so the change does not establish a shift from generalisation to capacity limits. The held-out POD/oracle ratios remain comparable (\nNsDataPodOverOracleTzero {} at $t=0$, \nNsDataPodOverOracleEvolved {} on evolved times). The $\nNsDataFactor \times $-data arm's B-DATA gate passed by the pre-registered value path (a hash mismatch on its node, at $10^{-16}$ on the archived trajectories). On the lower-dimensional family every error falls \nNsFamDropMin --\nNsFamDropMax $\times $ (POD-16 \nNsFamDropPod , oracle \nNsFamDropOracle , bank floor \nNsFamDropFloor ) while the ratio stays at \nNsFamOracleRatio {} (\nNsFamPodOverOracleTzero {} at $t=0$, \nNsFamPodOverOracleEvolved {} on evolved times) and the held-out/training ratio at \nNsFamHeldoutOverTrain {} against \nNsFamBaseGap . Oracle values are upper bounds: \nNsBudgetExitsKsixteen {} ($K{=}16$), \nNsBudgetExitsKthirtyTwo {} ($K{=}32$), \nNsFamBudgetExits {} (family dimension \nNsFamDimLow ) and \nNsDataBudgetExits {} (\nNsDataTrainN {} trajectories) held-out fits at $256^2$ hit the LM budget, which biases the ratio downward, so the result establishes failure to clear the gate with the available fits, not a proof that the globally best manifold fit cannot clear it}{table.caption.71}{}}
